-- title:
--   Components of the special fibre stay distinct over O
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $\mathfrak{X}$ be a term of the structure `DRModelPackage p`, which bundles data and properties for the integral model `DRModel p` — the two-chart integral model over $\mathbb{Z}$ of the full modular function field at level $p$ with respect to the Igusa element `IgusaScheme.jFull p` — including properness, flatness and integrality of `DRModel.toBase p`, integral closedness on affine opens, curve models over $\mathbb{Q}$ and over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ identified with the corresponding base changes together with Galois and place compatibilities, two sections `εinf`, `εzero` of `DRModel.toBase p`, a distinguished open `smoothLocus` maximal among opens smooth of relative dimension $1$ over the base, and further data, among them for each field a curve `𝔛.ratModel` and two morphisms `𝔛.compInf`, `𝔛.compZero` into the base-changed model. Let $O$ be a local ring whose maximal ideal equals the ideal generated by the image of $p$, let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and let $\mathrm{to}\kappa \colon O \to \kappa$ be a ring homomorphism (no locality is imposed). Write `DRModel.baseChangeMap` $\mathrm{to}\kappa$ for the induced morphism from the pullback of `DRModel.toBase p` along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathbb{Z} \to \kappa$ to the pullback along $\mathbb{Z} \to O$, obtained from the identity of `DRModel p` and $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{to}\kappa)$. The assertion is that the images, under the underlying continuous maps of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathtt{compInf}\,\kappa$ and of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathtt{compZero}\,\kappa$ each followed by this base-change morphism, of the generic point of the scheme underlying $\mathfrak{X}.\mathtt{ratModel}\,\kappa$ are distinct points of the model over $O$.
--
--   This records that the two components of the geometric special fibre of the Deligne–Rapoport model of $X_0(p)$ remain distinct after pushing forward to the model over $O$, a local ring with maximal ideal $(p)$. It underlies the component bookkeeping for the resolved model, and is cited by the results on branch ideals at the two sections and on existence of crossing and residue-field points.
